1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:03,600
Capacity: measure before you scale. "Will it scale?" has no answer without a workload.

2
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:12,000
Eighteen requests take 20 ms, one takes 500, one takes 1,000.

3
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:14,800
The mean is 93 ms. Sounds quick.

4
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:17,300
Nearest-rank 95th percentile: rank 19 of the sorted values is 500 ms.

5
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:20,300
Same data, two stories. Name the percentile method, sample size, and window.

6
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:22,600
Throughput (requests per second) is a different measurement again.

7
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:25,600
So is the working set: the data and index pages the workload keeps touching, versus memory.

8
00:00:28,100 --> 00:00:33,100
Each option relieves a different pressure and leaves a decision behind.

9
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:35,800
Don't shard a bad query just to run the bad query on more machines.

10
00:00:36,300 --> 00:00:38,500
For a 75-record catalog, the honest answer is tuning, or keeping it small.

11
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:45,300
A replica set: every member keeps the same 75 logical records. Protection, not three times the room.

12
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:50,400
Three shards: each OWNS a portion. Still one logical collection of 75.

13
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:53,700
Each portion still needs protection from member failure. The even split is only an illustration.

14
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:02,200
The multiplier is an assumption, not a billing rule. Retention and growth can change everything.

15
00:01:02,700 --> 00:01:05,000
1 MiB = 1,048,576 bytes; 1 MB = 1,000,000. Keep units explicit against a quota.

16
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:16,600
Remember: Latency (one request) and throughput (work per second) differ. Report a percentile with its method: the mean hides the tail. Remove waste first; every scaling step leaves a new job behind. Replicas are copies. Shards are portions, each usually replicated. Projections are assumptions to measure and revise.

